title: Turns taken through notes are a Delphi round, not a meeting, and that is their strength.
pid: de9aaec7-2152-4f79-aca3-e1950364dd3b
body: |2-
  Read in a summary: the Delphi method (RAND, 1950s) has experts
  answer apart, in writing, in rounds, each then seeing the others'
  answers with reasons, so that no reputation dominates and no
  bandwagon forms. Read in summaries: groups pool unique knowledge
  better when members know who is expert in what (Stasser, Stewart and
  Wittenbaum 1995); the team note's territories and each owner's map
  are that knowledge. Inferred: a mission that reads an earlier
  proposal anchors on it, so the second proposer must not read the
  first; and a second round costs two more missions for a method whose
  record is mixed, so there is one.
  Untested here; the trial is in [[id:0b103101-28e3-4380-8d26-e95a34f18dd5][what I am unsure of]].
